    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, the refrigerant absorbs heat and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. That heat escapes through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge. Next, the refrigerant cools down and turns back into a liquid, ready to soak up more heat. This cycle repeats over and over. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold, and your food stays fresh."
